WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.820
TLS adds a secure channel on top of transport I/O, but it does not eliminate normal networking behavior.

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:20.040
Handshakes can fail because of certificate problems, hostname mismatch, unsupported policy, network interruption, clock problems, missing trust anchors, peer shutdown, or configuration drift.

3
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:26.220
A robust integration expects these failures and maps them to safe, observable outcomes.

4
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:34.100
Encrypted reads and writes may still be partial or require retry according to the library's I/O model.

5
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:42.840
Nonblocking sockets, event loops, and asynchronous frameworks need state machines that preserve progress without treating incomplete data as valid.

6
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:54.140
The TLS layer may need to read while the application intended to write, or write while the application intended to read, depending on handshake and renegotiation behavior supported by the library.

7
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.900
TLS shutdown should be deliberate.

8
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:05.080
Closing an underlying socket is not always the same as completing the secure-channel shutdown sequence expected by the library and protocol policy.

9
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:13.180
The integration should define how normal completion, peer close, timeout, protocol error, and application cancellation are represented.

10
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.560
Cleanup should release resources without creating ambiguity about whether data was fully exchanged.

11
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.560
Errors should never silently downgrade security or switch to plaintext.

12
00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:31.260
If the secure channel cannot be established or maintained under policy, the connection should fail safely.

13
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:42.720
Diagnostics should help engineers distinguish validation failures, policy mismatches, transport failures, and application cancellations while avoiding secret material and sensitive payloads.

14
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.480
This is where state modeling pays off.

15
00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:57.980
A connection should have clear states for handshake, active encrypted communication, shutdown, and failure so retry logic and cleanup do not accidentally continue with unclear security properties.
